---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>The Flagship | Feels Like Om</title>
    <meta name="description" content="The Flagship — a small-group field immersion in West Sikkim with community learning, reflection and a 4D3N mountain trek." />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/type-system.css" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <header class="site-header">
      <a class="brand" href="/">Feels Like Om</a>
      <nav class="main-nav" aria-label="Main navigation">
        <a href="/about/our-story">ABOUT</a>
        <a class="active" href="/journeys">JOURNEYS</a>
        <a href="/reflections">REFLECTIONS</a>
        <a href="/contact">CONTACT</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <div class="hero-image" role="img" aria-label="A person looking across the mountains of West Sikkim beneath a dramatic sky"></div>
        <div class="hero-overlay"></div>
        <div class="hero-copy">
          <p class="eyebrow">West Sikkim | Field Immersion | 4D3N Trek</p>
          <h1>The Flagship</h1>
          <div class="hero-dates" aria-label="Upcoming Flagship dates">
            <p>Nov 22 - Dec 2, 2026</p>
            <p>Dec 22 - Jan 1, 2027</p>
          </div>
          <a class="button hero-cta" href="#enquire">Enquire about The Flagship</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="opening-quote" aria-label="Opening reflection">
        <blockquote>
          <span>&ldquo;We don&rsquo;t see things as they are;</span>
          <span>we see them as we are.&rdquo;</span>
          <cite>— Anaïs Nin</cite>
        </blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="program-intro" aria-label="About the program">
        <div class="program-intro-inner">
          <div class="program-images" aria-label="Journey image placeholders">
            <div class="image-placeholder program-image program-image-large"><span>Community / place photograph</span></div>
            <div class="image-placeholder program-image program-image-small"><span>Hands-on journey photograph</span></div>
          </div>
          <p class="program-row program-row-left">Equal parts community immersion and time in the mountains. It was designed by looking at what tourists usually do in Sikkim and inviting people into life beyond the overcrowded tourist circuit.</p>
          <p class="program-row program-row-right">Ease yourself into how life is lived in Yuksom. The days are unhurried, but your hands are busy and your heart is full. Churn butter, forage in the forest, learn the art of bamboo weaving.</p>
          <p class="program-row program-row-center">Let your curiosities guide the way you interact with the land, the people, and yourself.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="community-quote" aria-label="Community introduction">
        <blockquote>Meet the community who fought against a commercial company from exploiting the land.</blockquote>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-section-place">
        <div class="story-media image-placeholder tall"><span>Yuksom / community photograph</span></div>
        <div class="story-copy">
          <h2>Where you’ll be</h2>
          <p>Yuksom, West Sikkim. Gateway to the Khangchendzonga mountain range. Home to a community whose values have been shaped by the mountains they call home.</p>
          <p>Learn from the farmers, craftspeople, neighbours, aunties and uncles you will meet during the journey.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="story-section story-section-reflection">
        <div class="story-copy">
          <h2>Hold space for yourself</h2>
          <p>Reflection sharpens your awareness of your inner world. Don’t underestimate the power of collective energy that comes from being in a supportive environment (even just for 11 days).</p>
          <p>Allow yourself to be seen. Be heard.</p>
          <p>Reflection will be an ongoing part of your journey. There will be intentional time+space for you to notice what is happening around you and what is happening within you.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="story-media image-placeholder tall"><span>Reflection / quiet moment photograph</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="section rhythm" aria-label="The Flagship itinerary">
        <div class="wide">
          <div class="days-grid">
            <article><span>Day 1</span><h3>Arrival & Welcome</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 2</span><h3>Knowing our roots</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 3</span><h3>Everything is related</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 4</span><h3>Soil biome is gut biome</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 5</span><h3>Wildcard / Free & easy day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 6</span><h3>Start of trek</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 7</span><h3>Summit day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 8</span><h3>Rest day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 9</span><h3>Descend day</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 10</span><h3>Nature therapy</h3></article>
            <article><span>Day 11</span><h3>Not Goodbye</h3></article>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section editorial-grid">
        <div class="copy-block">
          <h2>Where you will stay</h2>
          <p>Ejam Residency is a three-storey home in Yuksom. The Ejam family lives on the second floor.</p>
          <p>You’ll know why I insist on putting Feels Like Om guests here.</p>
          <p>There are private bathrooms, hot showers, laundry (and WIFI) — but past guests speak much more fondly of conversations shared with the family in the kitchen, mornings on the lawn, afternoons in the library.</p>
          <p>One guest’s favourite morning ritual: taking huge mouthfuls of fresh air from the balcony.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="image-stack">
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Ejam Residency photograph</span></div>
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Room / kitchen / lawn photograph</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section food-grid">
        <div class="image-stack">
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Breakfast photograph</span></div>
          <div class="image-placeholder"><span>Dinner / local food photograph</span></div>
        </div>
        <div class="copy-block">
          <h2>Nourish your gut, nourish your soul</h2>
          <p>Breakfast is a grand affair.</p>
          <p>Buckwheat pancakes one morning, <em>alu puri</em> the next. The spread changes daily. There is always fresh fruit, hot tea and coffee.</p>
          <p class="pull">Dinner is omakase. Food you can't find in restaurants.</p>
          <p><em>Cowrie. Hand-rolled gnochhi-like dumplings in hot, savoury soup.</em></p>
          <p><em>Iskus ko munta. The first shoots of the chayote — a plant that keeps giving.</em></p>
          <p><em>Nettle soup. Fiddlehead ferns. Edible flowers.</em></p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section trek-overview" aria-labelledby="trek-overview-title">
        <div class="wide trek-overview-inner">
          <h2 id="trek-overview-title">Into the mountains</h2>

          <div class="trek-stats" aria-label="Trek overview statistics">
            <article class="trek-stat">
              <h3>Distance</h3>
              <p>~6 km one way</p>
            </article>
            <article class="trek-stat">
              <h3>Elevation gain</h3>
              <p>~1,300 m over two days</p>
            </article>
            <article class="trek-stat">
              <h3>Highest point</h3>
              <p>3,340 m</p>
            </article>
          </div>

          <figure class="trek-chart" aria-label="Elevation profile from approximately 2,100 metres at the start to 3,340 metres at 6 kilometres">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 1100 580" role="img" aria-labelledby="trek-chart-title trek-chart-desc">
              <title id="trek-chart-title">Trek elevation profile</title>
              <desc id="trek-chart-desc">The trail climbs from about 2,100 metres at zero kilometres to 2,620 metres at two kilometres, 3,120 metres at four kilometres, and 3,340 metres at six kilometres. The first two sections are steeper than the final section.</desc>
              <g class="chart-grid">
                <line x1="90" y1="470" x2="1040" y2="470" />
                <line x1="90" y1="390" x2="1040" y2="390" />
                <line x1="90" y1="310" x2="1040" y2="310" />
                <line x1="90" y1="230" x2="1040" y2="230" />
                <line x1="90" y1="150" x2="1040" y2="150" />
                <line x1="90" y1="70" x2="1040" y2="70" />
                <line x1="90" y1="70" x2="90" y2="470" />
                <line x1="407" y1="70" x2="407" y2="470" />
                <line x1="723" y1="70" x2="723" y2="470" />
                <line x1="1040" y1="70" x2="1040" y2="470" />
              </g>

              <g class="chart-axes">
                <line x1="90" y1="470" x2="1040" y2="470" />
                <line x1="90" y1="70" x2="90" y2="470" />
              </g>

              <path class="chart-area" d="M90 447 L407 308 L723 175 L1040 116 L1040 470 L90 470 Z" />
              <polyline class="chart-line" points="90,447 407,308 723,175 1040,116" />

              <g class="chart-points">
                <circle cx="90" cy="447" r="7" />
                <circle cx="407" cy="308" r="7" />
                <circle cx="723" cy="175" r="7" />
                <circle cx="1040" cy="116" r="7" />
              </g>

              <g class="chart-labels">
                <text x="90" y="505" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
                <text x="407" y="505" text-anchor="middle">2</text>
                <text x="723" y="505" text-anchor="middle">4</text>
                <text x="1040" y="505" text-anchor="middle">6</text>

                <text x="72" y="474" text-anchor="end">2000</text>
                <text x="72" y="394" text-anchor="end">2400</text>
                <text x="72" y="314" text-anchor="end">2800</text>
                <text x="72" y="234" text-anchor="end">3200</text>
                <text x="72" y="154" text-anchor="end">3400</text>
                <text x="72" y="74" text-anchor="end">3600</text>

                <text x="565" y="552" text-anchor="middle" class="axis-title">Distance (km)</text>
                <text x="24" y="270" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 24 270)" class="axis-title">Elevation (m)</text>

                <text x="170" y="440">2,100 m</text>
                <text x="390" y="285">2,620 m</text>
                <text x="705" y="152">3,120 m</text>
                <text x="1022" y="93" text-anchor="end">3,340 m</text>

                <text x="245" y="452" text-anchor="middle" class="section-label">SECTION 1</text>
                <text x="565" y="452" text-anchor="middle" class="section-label">SECTION 2</text>
                <text x="882" y="452" text-anchor="middle" class="section-label">SECTION 3</text>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </figure>

          <div class="trek-copy">
            <p class="trek-lead">This is a quiet trail, and we intend to keep it that way.</p>

            <p>A two-day climb, moving slowly through forest, bamboo and rhododendron before reaching the higher, more open landscape.</p>

            <p>The trek covers approximately <strong>6 km one way</strong>, with around <strong>1,300 m of elevation gain spread across two days</strong>. The first two sections are steeper, while the final stretch eases slightly as you approach the top.</p>

            <p>This is not a technical trek, but it is physically demanding. Expect <strong>several hours of uphill walking each day</strong>, uneven forest trails, roots, mud and occasional rocky sections. Conditions can become slippery after rain. Generally, most people consider this a moderate to challenging trek.</p>

            <p><strong>The terrain consists of</strong> forested mountain trail, with earth, roots, bamboo, rhododendron and steeper uphill sections. The trail becomes more open as you gain elevation.</p>

            <h3 class="trek-subheading">This trek may be for you if</h3>

            <p>You are comfortable walking uphill for several hours, enjoy being outdoors in changing weather, and do not mind uneven or muddy trails. You do not need technical climbing experience, but a reasonable level of fitness and willingness to move slowly and steadily are important.</p>

            <p>The pace is intentionally unhurried. There will be space to stop, rest and take in the landscape rather than treating the trail as something to conquer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section reflections">
        <div class="wide">
          <h2>Reflections from past participants</h2>
          <div class="reflection-grid">
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
            <div class="image-placeholder square"><span>Participant photograph</span></div>
          </div>
          <a class="text-link" href="/reflections">Read more reflections →</a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section faq" aria-labelledby="faq-title">
        <div class="narrow">
          <h2 id="faq-title">Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <div class="faq-list">
            <details>
              <summary>Who is this journey for?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <ul>
                  <li>You are curious about the world</li>
                  <li>You want to do more than sightseeing</li>
                  <li>You learn by doing, experiencing, and reflecting</li>
                  <li>You want to experience culture as a participant rather than a spectator</li>
                </ul>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>Do I need to be an experienced trekker?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Prior hiking experience will help, but it isn’t strictly necessary. We take short breaks and move at a pace the group can sustain. A reasonable level of fitness and stamina is required. Technical climbing skills are not required, but good balance — especially while descending rocky terrain — is helpful.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>How comfortable is camping?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>Your well-being is very important to us. We provide A-frame tents, four-inch mattresses, down sleeping bags, camping pillows, fleece blankets, hot water bags, a dining tent with tables and chairs, a toilet tent with a commode, and multi-course meals three times a day.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>What do I need to carry during the trek?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>You only need to carry a light daypack with your water bottle, snacks, and an extra layer. The dzos carry the rest, which is why we ask you to pack only what you genuinely need.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>How does altitude acclimatisation work?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>For first-timers to mountainous areas, it is normal to get breathless more quickly than usual when walking uphill or climbing stairs. The few days we spend in Yuksom before the trek should give your body time to gradually adjust before we start the trek.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
            <details>
              <summary>How big is the group?</summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>The Flagship is designed for a small group of 2–6 people. We keep the group intentionally small so there is space for connection, reflection and meaningful time with the community.</p>
              </div>
            </details>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="enquire" class="section enquire">
        <div class="narrow center">
          <h2>Curious about The Flagship?</h2>
          <p>If you want to understand the journey in more detail, tell us you’re interested.</p>
          <p>We’ll send you the full Flagship brochure with the detailed itinerary, trek information, inclusions, preparation notes and everything else you need to decide whether this journey is for you.</p>
          <a class="button" href="/contact">Enquire about The Flagship →</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>

<style>
  :global(*) { box-sizing: border-box; }
  :global(html) { scroll-behavior: smooth; }
  body { margin:0; font-family:var(--font-body); background:#f4efe5; color:#18301f; }
  h1,h2,h3,blockquote,.brand,.pull { font-family:var(--font-display); }
  p { line-height:1.7; }
  .site-header { position:absolute; top:0; left:0; right:0; z-index:5; display:flex; justify-content:space-between; align-items:center; gap:2rem; padding:1.5rem 4%; color:white; }
  .brand { color:inherit; text-decoration:none; white-space:nowrap; }
